= Solution
{parent=ii}

**False.** Give $X=\mathbb R$ its usual <topology>, identify all points of $(0,1)$ with one another, and leave every other point in a <singleton> <equivalence class>. Take the <closed set> $K=\{1/2\}$.

Its image $p(K)$ is a <singleton> in the <quotient space>, but its full <preimage>, also called the <saturation of a subset>, is
$$
p^{-1}(p(K))=(0,1).
$$
This is not <closed> in $\mathbb R$, since $0$ belongs to its <closure>. In the <quotient topology>, a <subset> is <closed> exactly when its <preimage> is <closed>, by applying the defining open-set rule to complements. Thus $p(K)$ is not <closed>, and this <quotient map> is not a <closed map>.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**True.** Choose the <irrational number> $\alpha=\sqrt2$, and partition the <rational numbers> into
$$
C_n=\mathbb Q\cap(n+\alpha,n+1+\alpha),\qquad n\in\mathbb Z.
$$
No endpoint is <rational>, so these disjoint sets cover $\mathbb Q$. Each is nonempty by the <density of the rational numbers>, and each is <clopen> in the <subspace topology> on $\mathbb Q$.

Let two <rational numbers> be equivalent exactly when they belong to the same $C_n$. The <bijection>
$$
h:\mathbb Q/{\sim}\longrightarrow\mathbb Z,\qquad h([q])=n\text{ when }q\in C_n
$$
is well-defined. For every <subset> $B\subseteq\mathbb Z$, the <preimage> of $h^{-1}(B)$ under the <canonical projection of a quotient set> is $\bigcup_{n\in B}C_n$, which is <open> in $\mathbb Q$. Thus every <subset> of $\mathbb Q/{\sim}$ is <open>: its <quotient topology> is the <discrete topology>. The usual <subspace topology> on the <integers> is also the <discrete topology>, because $\{n\}=\mathbb Z\cap(n-1/2,n+1/2)$. Consequently $h$ is a <homeomorphism>.

= iv
{parent=b}
